#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151834 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152058 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2755c3f-e76f-5cf9-a498-333e7e98ea8d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Addition and Subtraction Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "38f80c3a-6e6e-503d-a015-7040edc622bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-14580" :question/problem "If $x + 8 \\equiv 11\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,15)$, find an equivalent statement where $x$ is isolated." :question/worked-solution "The addition property of congruences states that\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+c\\equiv b+c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the subtraction property states that\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a-c\\equiv b-c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nWe use the subtraction property to subtract $8$ from both sides of the given congruence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 8 & ≡11 & (mod15) \\\\ x + 8 - 8 & ≡11 - 8 & (mod15) \\\\ x & ≡3 & (mod15)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151836 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152059 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "489ba3cf-c7da-5766-9638-2b5c5429d04c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integers That Are Congruent to Zero Modulo N"}] :question/id #uuid "e379f1ee-3de3-56ad-8866-d9189f892704" :question/math-academy-id "e-14582" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers is congruent to $0$ modulo $18$?\n\n1. $-45$\n2. $42$\n3. $-54$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = kn\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in\\mathbb Z$. In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n$, there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $18$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $18$.\n\nSo, since $18\\, \\mid \\,(-54)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n-54 \\equiv (-3) \\cdot {\\color{blue}18} \\equiv (-3)\\cdot 0\\equiv 0 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}18})\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $18$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151839 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152060 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7faa3c23-fa98-5c95-9c09-f0d489dbd472" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Congruences"}] :question/id #uuid "e92680c1-e40a-521d-b5b5-16a801e016ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-14585" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $x \\equiv 23\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$?\n\n1. $x \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n2. $x \\equiv 3 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n3. $x \\equiv 5 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$" :question/worked-solution "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n$. This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n$.\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that ${\\color{blue}6} \\cdot (-3) \\equiv 0 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}6})$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\nx + 0\\begin{vmatrix}≡23 + 0 & (mod6) \\\\ x & ≡23 + 0\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); ≡23 + 6 \\cdot (-3)\\begin{vmatrix}(mod6) \\\\ ≡23 - 18\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); ≡5 \\mid (mod6)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151863 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152061 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea9b0644-090a-52a4-bc21-18fee98acbd3" :knowledge-point/title "Date Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "1da5794f-02be-5a46-9450-d4b5611ee5a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14646" :question/problem "The opening ceremony of the first modern Olympic Games was held on April 6th, 1896. Given that January 1st, 1896, was a Wednesday, use congruences to determine the day on which the Olympic Games opened.\n\n*Hint: The year 1896 was a leap year since it's divisible by $4$ and is not an end-of-century year.*" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1896, and April 6th, 1896.\n\n- There were $2$ months with $31$ days (January and March).\n- There were $0$ months with $30$ days.\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February) since it was a leap year.\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and April 6th is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\overbrace{31(2) + 30(0) + 29(1)}^{\\textrm{days to April 1st}} + 5\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n$31 \\equiv 3, \\qquad 30\\equiv 2, \\qquad 29\\equiv 1 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\, 7)$.\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= 2(31) + 0(30) + 1(29) + 5 \\\\\n&= ≡2(3) + 0(2) + 1(1) + 5\\begin{vmatrix}(mod7) \\\\ ≡6 + 0 + 1 + 5\\end{vmatrix}(mod7) \\\\\n&= ≡12\\begin{vmatrix}(mod7) \\\\ ≡5\\end{vmatrix}(mod7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $5$ days after January 1st, 1896, namely Monday."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152058 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2755c3f-e76f-5cf9-a498-333e7e98ea8d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Addition and Subtraction Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8401b0d0-d84b-52c4-89e0-ab9576e51e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149384} {:answer/id #uuid "8a4422cf-bbc3-5f12-b258-04291aeea09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149385} {:answer/id #uuid "1b499b51-c2e1-5deb-922b-cf868455258d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149386}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d4205064-8335-5bff-bb4b-f85ca0f51fda" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149383} 